// Structural integrity (structural.rs). These four numbers between them
// decide how thin a cave roof gets before it comes down, how far an overhang
// reaches, and how tall a wall stands -- so they are the first place to look
// when building or mining feels wrong.
//
// max_unsupported_span was 3, the plan's own original suggestion ("stone 3,
// wood 8, steel 20"), back when it was carrying the entire model on its own
// and terrain had to be exempted from checking to survive it. It now means
// only "how far an *unconfined* cantilever reaches" -- confinement handles
// bulk rock -- which is a much narrower claim and affords a much larger
// number.
(
    name: "stone",
    display: "Stone",
    kind: Solid,
    density: 2.5,
    colors: [
        (128, 128, 132),
        (118, 118, 124),
        (138, 136, 140),
        (110, 112, 118),
    ],
    max_unsupported_span: 16,

    // Stone is a **product** of a reaction now (lava quenched by water), and
    // README.md's rule for that is unconditional: any target of
    // `burns_into`/`melts_into`/`boils_into`/a reaction inherits the source's
    // temperature and needs somewhere to put it. Left at the default 0 the
    // crust arrives at the lava's own 1000C and can *never* cool -- with no
    // conductivity, no melting point and no reactions, `fire::update` takes
    // its thermally-inert fast path and returns before it ever reads the
    // temperature again, so the cell is not merely slow to settle, it is
    // frozen hot for the rest of the run.
    //
    // Measured rather than assumed, because it is not the awake-chunk cost
    // that note warns about -- it is worse. A quenched flow in
    // `filmstrip scene=lavapour`, with this line absent: the crust becomes a
    // permanent 1000C boiler sitting in the pond. `reacted` stops at 811 by
    // frame ~1100 (all the lava is spent), and `boiled` keeps climbing
    // *linearly forever* -- 24,803 at frame 1100, 61,851 at frame 2300, a
    // steady ~30 boil/condense pairs every frame with nothing left burning
    // and nothing left flowing -- while 10 of 40 chunks never sleep again.
    // It reads on screen too: the crust draws tan, not grey, because
    // `render.rs`'s heat glow blends a 1000C cell halfway to its bright fire
    // tint whatever the material is.
    //
    // With this line: `boiled` converges (see the commit that added lava for
    // the paired numbers) and the crust cools to grey rock. The cost is the
    // real trade -- stone is the most numerous material in the world, and a
    // nonzero conductivity takes every *visited* stone cell off that fast
    // path and onto four neighbour temperature reads. `examples/ascii.rs`
    // has no stone in its stress scenes, so it cannot see that cost at all;
    // it was measured on the stone-heavy scenes instead. 0.1, matching
    // `ash.ron` (the other combustion product) rather than a physical basalt
    // figure, and well under `diffuse_heat`'s 0.25 stability ceiling.
    heat_conductivity: 0.1,

    // Attached rock -- the massif the slice is cut through -- is braced by
    // material out of plane, so it reaches far further than anything the
    // player stacks in front of it. 16 * 12 = 192 cells, comfortably more
    // than any span in a 512-wide world, while still being finite: undercut
    // a shelf far enough and it does come down. Loose foreground stone stays
    // on the bare 16, which is what makes a built cantilever a real limit.
    attached_span_bonus: 12,
    breaks_into: "rubble",

    // How coarsely stone comes apart: rungs on `rigid::fracture`'s
    // power-of-two ladder, so 6 means fragments of 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 or 64
    // cells. One rung more than the engine default, because stone is the
    // material a player spends the most time breaking and the top of the
    // ladder is what reads as "a piece of the wall came off" rather than
    // "the wall crumbled". Raise it for rock that should calve slabs;
    // lower it for anything that should shatter into grit.
    fragment_rungs: 6,


    // Rock is strong in compression, weak in bending, weakest in tension.
    // Leaning out costs the full step; hanging costs triple, so nothing
    // dangles far from what holds it.
    //
    // `support_cost_below` was 0 -- standing on rock was free -- and that
    // is deleted rather than tuned. It existed only to stop towers snapping
    // under the *reach* model, where a column's height counted against it
    // exactly like a cantilever's overhang. Under load a tower stands
    // because it *carries little* (its mass sits directly above it, so its
    // bending moment is ~0), which is the real reason, and the zero was
    // actively harmful for three separate reasons: whole regions shared one
    // distance, so "closer to an anchor" stopped ordering support and the
    // parent relation could cycle; a floating blob where every cell claimed
    // support from the one below sat at a self-consistent distance of 0 and
    // never even scheduled a recheck; and equal-cost ties made subtree
    // accumulation double-count. See `load.rs` and
    // `Reports/fracture-mechanics-design.md` §2/§3.1.
    support_cost_below: 1,
    support_cost_beside: 1,
    support_cost_above: 3,
)
